  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 145 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA cold front should have made its way through the area overnight \nFriday. The timing is in fairly good agreement between global models \nwith a five degree drop in the morning minimum temperatures compared \nto Friday. This will translate into a pleasant afternoon with highs \nin the upper 70s to low 80s. The northerly winds behind the front \nswap to southeasterly in the afternoon with the return flow. Aloft \nit is possible that a shortwave trough is passing through the area. \nThis shortwave would provide a little more lift and moisture to \nperhaps kick off scattered showers and thunderstorms with IVT in the \n90th percentile.\n\nSunday we should start to feel the effects of a new upper level \nsystem moving into the Pacific Northwest. Stronger return flow ahead \nof this system's passage would increase storm potential. Some \nmachine learning models are depicting a risk area on and east of I-\n25. Ensemble models have 75 percent of members having CAPE and shear \nvalues above climatological average and about 30-50 percent having \nvalues above the 90th percentile of climate. In other words the \nsignal for severe weather is starting to show in guidance. A cold \nfront associated with the Pacific system passes through sometime \nSunday night into Monday morning.\n\nPost front temperatures cool and a drying trend starts as a ridge \nbuilds up again. Return flow possible on Tuesday which could allow \nmore diurnal shower and storm activity. The signal for another \nsystem at this range is murky. If no other system turns up in \nensembles expect the ridging pattern to persist in the long\nterm.",
